/**
@class cv::vcucodec::RegionOfInterest

Region-of-interest encoding lets you steer the encoder's bit budget toward (or away from)
specific rectangular areas of the picture. Each region is assigned a quality relative to the
picture background, which the encoder translates into a per-LCU QP delta on top of the value
chosen by rate control. Regions may overlap and may be turned on and off at specific frames,
so ROIs can follow moving content over time.

A %RegionOfInterest is never constructed directly; it is created through one of the
@ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder factory methods, each of which returns a handle:
- @ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROI "createROI" — @copybrief cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROI
- @ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROIByValue "createROIByValue" — @copybrief cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROIByValue
- @ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROIBackground "createROIBackground" — @copybrief cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROIBackground
- @ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROIBackgroundByValue "createROIBackgroundByValue" — @copybrief cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROIBackgroundByValue

@note The **first** call to any of the ROI factory methods sets up the encoder's QP-table
path (the per-LCU QP-table buffers are allocated up front). Regions must therefore be created
**before encoding starts** — before the first @ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder::write "write()" or
@ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder::writeFile "writeFile()".

### Coordinates

A region is given as `(x, y, width, height)` in the picture's pixel coordinate system, whose
origin `(0, 0)` is the **top-left** corner: `x`/`y` are the top-left offset of the region and
`width` x `height` is its size. Positions and sizes are rounded outward to the encoder's LCU
grid. In Python a region is a plain `(x, y, width, height)` tuple; in C++ it is a `cv::Rect`.

@htmlonly
<div style="text-align:left; margin:1em 0;">
<svg width="560" height="350" viewBox="0 0 560 350" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
     font-family="sans-serif" font-size="13">
  <defs>
    <marker id="roiArwK" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="10" refX="8" refY="3"
            orient="auto-start-reverse" markerUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
      <path d="M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z" fill="#333"/></marker>
    <marker id="roiArwB" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="10" refX="8" refY="3"
            orient="auto-start-reverse" markerUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
      <path d="M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z" fill="#1f5fa8"/></marker>
    <marker id="roiArwR" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="10" refX="8" refY="3"
            orient="auto-start-reverse" markerUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
      <path d="M0,0 L8,3 L0,6 Z" fill="#c00"/></marker>
  </defs>

  <!-- frame -->
  <rect x="70" y="55" width="430" height="250" fill="#f4f6f8" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- region A (the one being measured) -->
  <rect x="250" y="150" width="170" height="110" fill="#4a90d9" fill-opacity="0.35"
        stroke="#1f5fa8" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="300" y="190" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f5fa8" font-weight="bold">A</text>
  <!-- region B, overlapping A -->
  <rect x="350" y="205" width="140" height="95" fill="#f0a94a" fill-opacity="0.40"
        stroke="#d9822b" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="484" y="293" text-anchor="end" fill="#c8760f" font-weight="bold">B</text>
  <!-- intersection of A and B -->
  <rect x="350" y="205" width="70" height="55" fill="#6f52a3" fill-opacity="0.45"
        stroke="#40306a" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>
  <text x="385" y="236" text-anchor="middle" fill="#2a1f47" font-weight="bold" font-size="11">overlap</text>

  <!-- origin -->
  <circle cx="70" cy="55" r="3.5" fill="#c00"/>
  <text x="64" y="50" text-anchor="end" fill="#c00">(0,0)</text>

  <!-- frame width -->
  <line x1="70" y1="38" x2="500" y2="38" stroke="#333" marker-start="url(#roiArwK)" marker-end="url(#roiArwK)"/>
  <text x="285" y="32" text-anchor="middle">frame width (PictureEncSettings.width)</text>
  <!-- frame height -->
  <line x1="50" y1="55" x2="50" y2="305" stroke="#333" marker-start="url(#roiArwK)" marker-end="url(#roiArwK)"/>
  <text x="44" y="180" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90 44 180)">frame height (PictureEncSettings.height)</text>

  <!-- x offset (frame-left -> ROI-left, along ROI top) -->
  <line x1="70" y1="150" x2="250" y2="150" stroke="#c00" stroke-dasharray="4 3"
        marker-start="url(#roiArwR)" marker-end="url(#roiArwR)"/>
  <text x="160" y="144" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c00">x</text>
  <!-- y offset (frame-top -> ROI-top, along ROI left) -->
  <line x1="250" y1="55" x2="250" y2="150" stroke="#c00" stroke-dasharray="4 3"
        marker-start="url(#roiArwR)" marker-end="url(#roiArwR)"/>
  <text x="258" y="107" text-anchor="start" fill="#c00">y</text>

  <!-- ROI width -->
  <line x1="250" y1="170" x2="420" y2="170" stroke="#1f5fa8" marker-start="url(#roiArwB)" marker-end="url(#roiArwB)"/>
  <text x="335" y="165" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f5fa8">width</text>
  <!-- ROI height -->
  <line x1="272" y1="150" x2="272" y2="260" stroke="#1f5fa8" marker-start="url(#roiArwB)" marker-end="url(#roiArwB)"/>
  <text x="279" y="210" text-anchor="start" fill="#1f5fa8">height</text>
</svg>
</div>
@endhtmlonly

Example: `createROI((x, y, width, height), quality)` — a `(1280, 360, 1220, 1500)` region starts
1280 px from the left and 360 px from the top of the frame and is 1220 x 1500 px in size. A second
region **B** is shown overlapping **A**; their intersection is highlighted (see @ref cv::vcucodec::ROIOrder "ROIOrder").

### Quality

The quality of a region (or of the background) is expressed with
@ref cv::vcucodec::ROIQuality "ROIQuality". Each named value maps to a relative QP delta applied
on top of the rate-control QP; a lower QP means better quality and more bits.

@anchor roi_quality_table
| ROIQuality  | QP delta | Meaning                                                           |
|:-----------:|:--------:|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `HIGH`      |    −5    | Higher quality than the rate-control value                        |
| `MEDIUM`    |     0    | Same quality as the rate-control value                            |
| `LOW`       |    +5    | Lower quality than the rate-control value                         |
| `DONT_CARE` |   +31    | Worst possible quality (region of no interest)                    |
| `STATIC`    |     —    | Region is coded as static content (same as the reference picture) |
| `INTRA`     |     —    | Region is coded entirely with Intra prediction                    |

When a named preset is not precise enough, use the *by-value* factories
(@ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROIByValue "createROIByValue" /
@ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROIBackgroundByValue "createROIBackgroundByValue") to pass an
explicit relative QP delta directly. The delta is a signed value (roughly −32..+31); it is
reported by @ref deltaQP.

### Lifecycle: create → enable → disable

A region is **created disabled** and has no effect on the bitstream until it is enabled. All
state changes are scheduled at a specific frame index, so they line up with the encoded stream:

- @ref enable "enable(frameIdx)" — activate the region starting at @p frameIdx.
- @ref disable "disable(frameIdx)" — deactivate the region starting at @p frameIdx.

Calling @ref disable is the deterministic way to stop a region contributing to the output.
Destroying the handle merely releases the region entry: in C++ this happens immediately (RAII);
from Python it happens when the object is garbage-collected, which is not deterministic.

A region's rectangle and quality are fixed at creation; to move or re-qualify a region, drop its
handle and create a new one.

To make a region first appear at a later frame, create it and enable it at that frame:

@code{.py}
    # a high-quality box that is active only for frames [100, 250)
    roi = enc.createROI((1280, 360, 1220, 1500), cv2.vcucodec.ROIQuality_HIGH)
    roi.enable(100)
    roi.disable(250)
@endcode

### Background

The picture **background** — every area not covered by an enabled region — is itself modelled as a
full-frame %RegionOfInterest, created via
@ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROIBackground "createROIBackground". Its @ref region reports the
whole picture. The background is created disabled like any other region and is activated with
@ref enable.

Only **one** background is effective at a time: enabling a background supersedes any
previously-enabled one (most-recently-enabled wins). When no background region is enabled, the
background is coded at the rate-control QP (equivalent to `MEDIUM`). Setting the background to
`LOW` or `DONT_CARE` frees bits that can then be spent on high-quality regions at a fixed bitrate.

### Overlap order

When enabled regions overlap, a single frame-global policy decides which region's quality applies
in the intersection, expressed with @ref cv::vcucodec::ROIOrder "ROIOrder":

- `QUALITY` — the highest-quality (lowest-QP) overlapping region wins.
- `INCOMING` — the earliest-defined region wins.

For the overlapping regions **A** and **B** in the Coordinates figure above, `QUALITY` codes
their intersection at whichever region has the better quality, while `INCOMING` codes it at
whichever region was created first.

The order can be provided when the background is created (the @p order argument of
@ref cv::vcucodec::Encoder::createROIBackground "createROIBackground"), or changed at runtime with
@ref setOrder "setOrder(frameIdx, order)".

@note @ref setOrder is a **frame-global** policy: although it is a method on a single region
handle, it affects how *all* overlapping regions are resolved, not just the region it is called on.

### Inspecting a region

- @ref region "region()" — the rectangle in pixels (the full frame for a background region).
- @ref enabled "enabled()" — whether the region is currently enabled.
- @ref quality "quality()" — the named quality (meaningful when not created "by value").
- @ref deltaQP "deltaQP()" — the relative QP delta (meaningful when created "by value").

### Python example

@code{.py}
    import cv2

    params = cv2.vcucodec.EncoderInitParams()
    params.pictureEncSettings.codec  = cv2.vcucodec.Codec_HEVC
    params.pictureEncSettings.width  = 1920
    params.pictureEncSettings.height = 1080
    enc = cv2.vcucodec.createEncoder("out.h265", params)

    # Create regions BEFORE encoding starts.
    bg  = enc.createROIBackground(cv2.vcucodec.ROIQuality_LOW,
                                  cv2.vcucodec.ROIOrder_QUALITY)
    roi = enc.createROI((1280, 360, 1220, 1500), cv2.vcucodec.ROIQuality_HIGH)
    hot = enc.createROIByValue((0, 0, 320, 240), -10)  # explicit QP delta

    bg.enable(0)      # background active from the start
    roi.enable(100)   # high-quality box appears at frame 100
    hot.enable(0)
    roi.disable(250)  # ... and disappears at frame 250

    # ... feed frames with enc.write(...) / enc.writeFile(...), then enc.eos()
@endcode

@see cv::vcucodec::Encoder, cv::vcucodec::ROIQuality, cv::vcucodec::ROIOrder
*/
